<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamics of a linear dynamical system over a finite field can be described by using the elementary divisors of the corresponding matrix. It is natural to extend the investigation to a general finite commutative ring. In a previous publication, the last two authors developed an efficient algorithm to determine whether a linear dynamical system over a finite commutative ring is a fixed point system or not. The algorithm can also be used to reduce the problem of finding the cycles of such a system to the case where the system is given by an automorphism. Here, we further analyze the cycle structure of such a system and develop a method to determine its cycles.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Finite rings, Modules, Linear systems, Cycle lengths
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>13M99, 13P99, 15B33
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative ring with <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module, and let <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\longrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\longrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module endomorphism. We may consider <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a dynamical system via iteration:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f,\;\;f^{2}=f\circ f,\;\;\cdots,\;\;f^{m}=\underbrace{f\circ f\circ\cdots\circ f}_{\mbox{$m$ copies}},\;\;\cdots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.449em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mtext> copies</mtext></mrow></munder></mrow><mo rspace="0.449em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f,\;\;f^{2}=f\circ f,\;\;\cdots,\;\;f^{m}=\underbrace{f\circ f\circ\cdots\circ f}_{\mbox{$m$ copies}},\;\;\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and investigate the behaviors of the system. For our convenience, we set <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{0}=id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{0}=id</annotation></semantics></math>. By studying the dynamics of the system, we mean to investigate its possible cycles (including fixed points) generated by the elements of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clear that if the cardinality of <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite, then there may not be any cycle except the obvious fixed point <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. But if <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, then for any initial input <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{m}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{m}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, will eventually either stabilize or enter a cycle. We should restrict our attention to the case where both <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are finite in this paper, and we call such a system <span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a linear dynamical system over the finite ring <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=F</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite field, then <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite dimensional vector space over <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, and the dynamics of <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be described by using the elementary divisors of the <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose the elementary divisors of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, pp. 356-357):</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{i}^{m_{ij}},\;\;1\leq i\leq s,\;\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>  1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>  1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{i}^{m_{ij}},\;\;1\leq i\leq s,\;\;1\leq j\leq k_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible polynomial in <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[x]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are integers such that for each <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{i1}\geq m_{i2}\geq\cdots\geq m_{ik_{i}}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{i1}\geq m_{i2}\geq\cdots\geq m_{ik_{i}}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Then there are <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-cyclic subspaces <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. there exists a vector <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,f(v),\ldots,f^{k}(v))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,f(v),\ldots,f^{k}(v))</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>) with minimal polynomial <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{m_{ij}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{m_{ij}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq s,\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq s,\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of the <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>’s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see (over any ring <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) that if <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=M_{1}\oplus M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=M_{1}\oplus M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum decomposition of <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant submodules (we will just call them <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules), and <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle in <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>), then the direct product</p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle C_{1}\times C_{2}:=\{(a,b)\;|\;a\in M_{1},\;b\in M_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{1}\times C_{2}:=\{(a,b)\;|\;a\in M_{1},\;b\in M_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">offers <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GCD(c_{1},c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GCD(c_{1},c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> cycles of length <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LCM(c_{1},c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LCM(c_{1},c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for the module <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. So we can just work with each direct summand independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the problem of finding the cycles (including fixed points) of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (over a field <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>) reduces to the problem of finding the cycles of the restricted linear maps</p>
<table id="S1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{ij}=f|_{M_{ij}}\;:\;M_{ij}\longrightarrow M_{ij},\;\;1\leq i\leq s,\;\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>  1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>  1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{ij}=f|_{M_{ij}}\;:\;M_{ij}\longrightarrow M_{ij},\;\;1\leq i\leq s,\;\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=x</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding linear maps <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij},\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij},\;1\leq j\leq k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, are nilpotent and thus all have just one fixed point <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and have no cycle of length <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. So the question is to find the cycle structures of those <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the corresponding <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are not <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (these <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are automorphisms of the <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>). For these cases, since the characteristic polynomial of each <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to its minimal polynomial, Elspas’ formula describes its cycle structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> (we use <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{m_{ij}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{m_{ij}}</annotation></semantics></math> to simplify the notation in the formula):</p>
<table id="S1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{ij}=1+\sum_{k=1}^{s}\frac{q^{mk}-q^{m(k-1)}}{c_{k}}\mathcal{C}_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{ij}=1+\sum_{k=1}^{s}\frac{q^{mk}-q^{m(k-1)}}{c_{k}}\mathcal{C}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=|F|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=|F|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the fixed point <math id="S1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle of length <math id="S1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}=\mbox{ord}(g_{ij}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ord</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}=\mbox{ord}(g_{ij}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the least positive integer <math id="S1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}-1</annotation></semantics></math>). This formula gives a nice description of the cycle structure, though the actual computation is more involved: one first computes the normal form of the matrix <math id="S1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xI-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xI-A</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix of <math id="S1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to a basis of <math id="S1.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, or computes its minimum polynomial <math id="S1.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(f)</annotation></semantics></math>, then factors the polynomials that appear in the normal form of <math id="S1.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xI-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xI-A</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S1.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(f)</annotation></semantics></math>) to get the <math id="S1.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then computes the order of each <math id="S1.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then all <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{ij}^{s},\;0\leq s\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}^{s},\;0\leq s\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, divide <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{t}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{t}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. So by the comment about the cycles of a direct sum of <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-module endomorphism <math id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> possesses a cycle of maximum length, say <math id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, such that all other cycle lengths are divisors of <math id="S1.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For a general finite commutative ring <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, since the factorization in <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R[x]</annotation></semantics></math> may not be unique, different approaches are needed. The study of linear dynamical systems over rings of the form <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> was suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> due to its relation with monomial dynamical systems over finite fields. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> an approach by using Fitting’s lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>: there is a positive integer <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mbox{Ker}f^{k}\oplus\mbox{Im}f^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Ker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mbox{Ker}f^{k}\oplus\mbox{Im}f^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> was suggested. However, the key to the success of the proposed approach, which is to find a proper <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and compute <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently, was not treated. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, an upper bound for <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> was determined, and an algorithm that runs in time <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3}\log(n\log(q)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3}\log(n\log(q)))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=R^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=|R|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=|R|</annotation></semantics></math>, to compute <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> was developed. The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> provide a solution to the problem of determining whether or not such a system is a fixed point system, and also reduce the problem of finding the cycle structure of such a system to that of an automorphism (since <math id="S1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mbox{Im}f^{k}\longrightarrow\mbox{Im}f^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mbox{Im}f^{k}\longrightarrow\mbox{Im}f^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is bijective).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> is, to determine the cycles of <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, we can just consider the <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as an abelian group (<math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-module) and determine the lengths of the cycles. After obtaining the information on the lengths of the cycles, we can find the cycles by solving linear systems of the form <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{c}-I)X=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{c}-I)X=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity map and <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle length. By the structure theorem of finitely generated abelian groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, we can assume that</p>
<table id="S1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{Im}f^{k}=\mathbb{Z}_{p_{1}^{a_{1}}}\oplus\mathbb{Z}_{p_{2}^{a_{2}}}\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathbb{Z}_{p_{m}^{a_{m}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{Im}f^{k}=\mathbb{Z}_{p_{1}^{a_{1}}}\oplus\mathbb{Z}_{p_{2}^{a_{2}}}\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathbb{Z}_{p_{m}^{a_{m}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\ldots,p_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\ldots,p_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are (not necessarily distinct) primes, and <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\ldots,a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\ldots,a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are (not necessarily distinct) positive integers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We further note that an <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-module as in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) can be written as a direct sum of <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules such that each of these submodules is formed by grouping those <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p_{i}^{a_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p_{i}^{a_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s with the same prime <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> together. As discussed before, whenever we have a direct sum of <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodules, we can just work with each direct summand independently. Therefore, our goal here is to analyze the cycle structure of an automorphism of an abelian group of the form (<math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime):</p>
<table id="S1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 17.07182ptM=\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{1}}}\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{2}}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{m}}},\;1\leq a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq\ldots\leq a_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 17.07182ptM=\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{1}}}\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{2}}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{m}}},\;1\leq a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq\ldots\leq a_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Since abelian groups are <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, an endomorphism of <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by an integer matrix <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>; and if all <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is an endomorphism of the vector space <math id="S1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> over the field <math id="S1.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the case where all <math id="S1.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are the same, i.e. <math id="S1.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p^{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p^{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>, an approach was developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> for an arbitrary endomorphism of <math id="S1.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> using number theory techniques based on congruence of integers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we consider the general case where <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) such that the <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are not necessary the same. Our emphasis is on the computation of the cycles; and for that, we need to be able to compute the possible cycle lengths efficiently. In Section 2, we give an upper bound for the cycle lengths such that all possible cycle lengths are factors of the given upper bound. The factors of the given upper bound are obtained from the induced linear systems over the finite field <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus there is no extra work needed to factor the upper bound. The results of this paper and the results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> together provide an algorithm to determine the cycles structure of a linear dynamical system over a finite commutative ring. The algorithm is given in Section 3. We consider three examples in Section 4. The first example shows that the given upper bound for the cycle lengths is sharp, and the third example is a linear system derived from a real world cancer regulatory network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Result</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by collecting some general facts. The following lemma holds for any ring <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,N</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, let <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:M\rightarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:M\rightarrow N</annotation></semantics></math> be an onto <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism, and let <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\ker\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\ker\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module endomorphism such that <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(K)\subset K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(K)\subset K</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> induces an <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module endomorphism <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.Thmlemma1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(\varphi(m))=\varphi(f(m)),\;m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(\varphi(m))=\varphi(f(m)),\;m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is a straightforward verification of the definition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that we work with a finite commutative ring <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and a finite <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module, so it is possible to use a reduction approach to find the cycles of an <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module endomorphism <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. For this purpose, we investigate the relationships among the cycles of the <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (notation as in Lemma 2). For any <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(m)</annotation></semantics></math> the length of the cycle generated by <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a cycle). We have the following simple fact about fixed points:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation as in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Let <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed point of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed point of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=v</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed point of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u_{1})=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u_{1})=v</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed point <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in K</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the number of fixed points <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=v</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or equal to the number of fixed points of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have that <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed point <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u+w)=u+f(w)=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u+w)=u+f(w)=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(w)=w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(w)=w</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From now on, we assume that <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is an automorphism. Then every element of <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> lies in a cycle. Note that by our assumption on <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a_{m}}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a_{m}}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> (see (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>)), thus <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a_{m}}(K)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a_{m}}(K)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is the least positive integer <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{i}(K)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{i}(K)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=v\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=v\in N</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(v)=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(v)=s</annotation></semantics></math>. With these assumptions, we have <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{s}(u)=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{s}(u)=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(w)=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(w)=k</annotation></semantics></math>. We will write <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s,k]</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,k)</annotation></semantics></math>) for the least common multiple (respectively, the greatest common divisor) of <math id="S2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmlemma2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists <math id="S2.Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq b\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq b\leq a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(u)=p^{b}[s,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(u)=p^{b}[s,k]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(u)=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(u)=c</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(u)=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(u)=u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}^{c}(v)=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}^{c}(v)=v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=ts" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=ts</annotation></semantics></math>, then from <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{s}(u)=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{s}(u)=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=f^{s}(u)-u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=f^{s}(u)-u</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u=f^{ts}(u)=f^{(t-1)s}(u)+f^{(t-1)s}(w)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u=f^{ts}(u)=f^{(t-1)s}(u)+f^{(t-1)s}(w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Applying <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to this equation we have <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(w)=w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(w)=w</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is also a multiple of <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a common multiple of <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="S2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=c_{0}[s,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=c_{0}[s,k]</annotation></semantics></math> for some positive integer <math id="S2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This holds without the assumption that <math id="S2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a}(K)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a}(K)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a}(K)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a}(K)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we will have <math id="S2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}=p^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}=p^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq b\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq b\leq a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">To see that, let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s,k]=ns" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s,k]=ns</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\frac{k}{(s,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\frac{k}{(s,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Start with <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{s}(u)=u+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{s}(u)=u+w</annotation></semantics></math> and compute inductively, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{ns}(u)=u+w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{ns}(u)=u+w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that since <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ns" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ns</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{ns}-I)(w)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{ns}-I)(w)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(f^{s}-I)(w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w))=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(f^{s}-I)(w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w))=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">that is</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{1}:=w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{1}:=w+f^{s}(w)+\cdots+f^{(n-1)s}(w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">is a fixed point of <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=[s,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=[s,k]</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we do not know if <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or not, so we use the assumption that <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a}(K)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a}(K)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Start from (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>): <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{ns}(u)=u+w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{ns}(u)=u+w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, note that <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed point of <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{p^{a}ns}(u)=(f^{ns})^{p^{a}}(u)=u+p^{a}w_{1}=u.\quad\mbox{(since $w_{1}\in K$)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(since </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{p^{a}ns}(u)=(f^{ns})^{p^{a}}(u)=u+p^{a}w_{1}=u.\quad\mbox{(since $w_{1}\in K$)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a factor of <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a}ns" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a}ns</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}=p^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}=p^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq b\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq b\leq a</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We now assume that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\longrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\longrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-module automorphism, where <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>). Note that <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a_{m}}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a_{m}}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</p>
<table id="S2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{i}=\{v\in M\;|\;p^{i}v=0\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{i}=\{v\in M\;|\;p^{i}v=0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Then each <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule filtration</p>
<table id="S2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{0}=(0)\subset M_{1}\subset\cdots\subset M_{a_{m}-1}\subset M_{a_{m}}=M." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{0}=(0)\subset M_{1}\subset\cdots\subset M_{a_{m}-1}\subset M_{a_{m}}=M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.3" class="ltx_p">There are positive integers <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}\leq m,\;1\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}\leq m,\;1\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the quotients</p>
<table id="S2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.4" class="ltx_p">The automorphism <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> induces automorphisms</p>
<table id="S2.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{f}_{i}\;:\;M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}\longrightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}},\;\;1\leq i\leq a_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>  1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{f}_{i}\;:\;M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}\longrightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}},\;\;1\leq i\leq a_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.5" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field, the dynamics of these automorphisms can be determined as discussed in the introduction section. For each <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, let the set of cycle lengths of <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{ij}=\{c_{ij}\;:\;1\leq j\leq\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{ij}=\{c_{ij}\;:\;1\leq j\leq\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>},
where <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer depending on <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}&gt;c_{ij},\;1\leq i\leq a_{m},\;1&lt;j\leq\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}&gt;c_{ij},\;1\leq i\leq a_{m},\;1&lt;j\leq\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, for any <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{ij}|c_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{ij}|c_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\longrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\longrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-module automorphism, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), and keep the notation introduced above.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(1) For each integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>), the dynamical system <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\longrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\longrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> possesses a cycle whose length is a multiple of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(2) All cycle lengths of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are factors of</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p^{a_{m}-1}\cdot\mbox{LCM}(c_{11},c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LCM</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p^{a_{m}-1}\cdot\mbox{LCM}(c_{11},c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where LCM stands for the least common multiple.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">(1) For a fixed <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that its image <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}\in M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}\in M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a cycle of length <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the cycle to which <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> belongs has length <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(u)=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(u)=u</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(\bar{u})=\overline{f^{c}(u)}=\bar{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(\bar{u})=\overline{f^{c}(u)}=\bar{u}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}|c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}|c</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">(2) We use induction on <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is nothing to prove if <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{m}=1</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is just an automorphism of the vector space <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Z}^{m}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{m}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{m}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then since <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> induces an automorphism of each <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), it induces an automorphism of</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle M/M_{1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{1}-1}}\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{2}-1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{m}-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M/M_{1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{1}-1}}\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{2}-1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_{m}-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">The filtration of (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) induces the following filtration:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 17.07182pt(0)=M_{1}/M_{1}\subset M_{2}/M_{1}\subset\cdots\subset M_{a_{m}-1}/M_{1}\subset M/M_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 17.07182pt(0)=M_{1}/M_{1}\subset M_{2}/M_{1}\subset\cdots\subset M_{a_{m}-1}/M_{1}\subset M/M_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since for <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(M_{i}/M_{1})/(M_{i-1}/M_{1})\cong M_{i}/M_{i-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(M_{i}/M_{1})/(M_{i-1}/M_{1})\cong M_{i}/M_{i-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">by the induction assumption, we have that the possible cycle lengths of <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are factors of <math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=p^{a_{m}-2}LCM(c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=p^{a_{m}-2}LCM(c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the theorem follows by noticing <math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(M_{1})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(M_{1})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and applying Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> to <math id="S2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to the projection <math id="S2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\rightarrow M/M_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\rightarrow M/M_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmcorollary1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the case where all <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal), the cycle lengths are factors of <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a-1}c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a-1}c</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum cycle length of <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/M_{a-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/M_{a-1}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with the module structure induced by <math id="S2.Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the onto <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:M\rightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:M\rightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by first taking <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\rightarrow p^{a-i}m,\;m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\rightarrow p^{a-i}m,\;m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, and then follows by the projection from <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This homomorphism induces an <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/M_{a-1}\rightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/M_{a-1}\rightarrow M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> since its kernel is <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{a-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{a-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus all <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}/M_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}/M_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\leq i\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\leq i\leq a</annotation></semantics></math>) are isomorphic as <math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and have the same cycle structure, which implies that <math id="S2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}=c</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corollary follows from (2) of the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that if <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not an automorphism then the above corollary does not apply, since not every element of <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> lies in a cycle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Computing the Cycles</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the assumption that the cycle lengths of a linear system over a finite field can be computed, we can use Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to find the cycles of a linear system <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\rightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\rightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> over a finite commutative ring <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Our algorithm is divided into four steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduce to an automorphism.</span> By Theorem 2.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                  
                  
                The assumption on the module considered in Theorem 2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> is somewhat different, but a quick check of the proof there reveals that it also applies to the case here.</span></span></span> <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=m\log_{2}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=m\log_{2}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{N+1}(M)=f^{N}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{N+1}(M)=f^{N}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=|R|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=|R|</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a presentation of the <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of the free module <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varphi:R^{m}\longrightarrow M\;\;\mbox{and}\;\;R^{m}/\ker(\varphi)\cong M." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varphi:R^{m}\longrightarrow M\;\;\mbox{and}\;\;R^{m}/\ker(\varphi)\cong M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore we have an induced automorphism <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:f^{N}(M)\rightarrow f^{N}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:f^{N}(M)\rightarrow f^{N}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the structure of the finitely generated abelian group <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{N}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{N}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, and reduce it to the case where the module is defined by (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>). Thus we assume the module <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) in steps 2 – 4.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute the number <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math> for each induced automorphism (see the paragraph precedes Theorem 1)</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{f}_{i}:\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}},1\leq i\leq a_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{f}_{i}:\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k_{i}},1\leq i\leq a_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If the minimal polynomial of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{i}=g_{i1}^{r_{i1}}\cdots g_{is_{i}}^{r_{is_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}=g_{i1}^{r_{i1}}\cdots g_{is_{i}}^{r_{is_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is its irreducible factorization, then <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}=LCM(h_{i1},\ldots,h_{is_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}=LCM(h_{i1},\ldots,h_{is_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}=ord(g_{ij}^{r_{ij}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}=ord(g_{ij}^{r_{ij}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field, one can use the existing approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. See also the discussions later.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute the order of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as an element of the automorphism group of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Let <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=p^{a_{m}-1}\cdot\mbox{LCM}(c_{11},c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>LCM</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=p^{a_{m}-1}\cdot\mbox{LCM}(c_{11},c_{21},\ldots,c_{a_{m}1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of the order of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the factorization of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is known from step 2 and step 3, we can use the following procedure to compute the order of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_t">Input:</th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> — a multiple of the order of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l">Output:</th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ord" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ord</annotation></semantics></math> — the order of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_t"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ord\leftarrow n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ord\leftarrow n</annotation></semantics></math>;</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">while ( 1 ) do</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ordersmaller\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ordersmaller\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>;</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">for ( each prime factor <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ord" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ord</annotation></semantics></math> )</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">if ( <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\frac{ord}{p}}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\frac{ord}{p}}=I</annotation></semantics></math> )</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.8.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ord\leftarrow\frac{ord}{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ord\leftarrow\frac{ord}{p}</annotation></semantics></math>;</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.9.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.9.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ordersmaller\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ordersmaller\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>;</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">break;</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">if ( <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ordersmaller=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ordersmaller=0</annotation></semantics></math> )</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_l"></th>
<td id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.12.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">return <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ord" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ord</annotation></semantics></math>;</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute the cycle lengths and the cycles.</span> Let the order of <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the cycle lengths of <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are given by the factors of <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are known from step 2. For a factor <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, solve the linear system <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{d}-I)X=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{d}-I)X=0</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the cycles of length <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of the algorithm.</span> We ignore the factors contributed by <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=|R|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=|R|</annotation></semantics></math> in our analysis, since for application purposes we can assume that <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In step 1, the computation of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> takes time <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, to find the structure of the abelian group <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{N}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{N}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, it requires to compute the Smith normal form of the integer matrix of <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:f^{N}(M)\rightarrow f^{N}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:f^{N}(M)\rightarrow f^{N}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that the computation of the Smith normal form takes time <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In step 3, the given algorithm computes the order of <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in time <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> since computing <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\mathbf{a}/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\mathbf{a}/p}</annotation></semantics></math> requires at most time <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{2}\sqrt{m}\log(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{2}\sqrt{m}\log(m))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The computations in step 4 are straightforward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since we could not find any readily accessible reference on the algorithm and its analysis for the case where <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field, we give a discussion of the complexity of step 2 as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">(1) There is a randomized algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to compute the minimal polynomial <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math> of a liner map
<math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in time <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and uses <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(k)</annotation></semantics></math> random vectors. Since <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, this computation requires expected time no worse than <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">(2) To factorize <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, some algorithms from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> can be used. Assume the degree of <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
and let <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of field operations needed for multiplying two polynomials
of degree at most <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be factorized in expected number of <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d{\bf M}(d)\log(dp))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d{\bf M}(d)\log(dp))</annotation></semantics></math> field
operations. Since <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, this requires time no worse than <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">(3) Note that if <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible polynomial with <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{ord}(g)=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ord</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{ord}(g)=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{ord}(g^{u})=\alpha p^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ord</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{ord}(g^{u})=\alpha p^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest integer such that <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{c}\geq u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{c}\geq u</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, our algorithm runs in expected time <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\tilde{O}(m^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\tilde{O}(m^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Conclusion and Examples</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have developed an efficient algorithm to determine the cycle structure of a linear dynamical system over a finite commutative ring with identity and thus provided a solution to this problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude our paper by considering three examples. The first example shows that the bound given by (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) is sharp.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmexample1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{Z}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{Z}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\mathbb{Z}_{4}\times\mathbb{Z}_{8}\times\mathbb{Z}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\mathbb{Z}_{4}\times\mathbb{Z}_{8}\times\mathbb{Z}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\longrightarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\longrightarrow M</annotation></semantics></math> be defined by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;1\\
0&amp;0&amp;1\\
\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;1\\
0&amp;0&amp;1\\
\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with respect to the generators <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=(1,0,0)^{t},\;e_{2}=(0,1,0)^{t},\;e_{3}=(0,0,1)^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=(1,0,0)^{t},\;e_{2}=(0,1,0)^{t},\;e_{3}=(0,0,1)^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{m}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{m}=4</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{0}=(0)\subset M_{1}=&lt;2e_{1},4e_{2},8e_{3}&gt;\subset M_{2}=&lt;e_{1},2e_{2},4e_{3}&gt;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{0}=(0)\subset M_{1}=&lt;2e_{1},4e_{2},8e_{3}&gt;\subset M_{2}=&lt;e_{1},2e_{2},4e_{3}&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\subset M_{3}=&lt;e_{1},e_{2},2e_{3}&gt;\subset M_{4}=M," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊂</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\subset M_{3}=&lt;e_{1},e_{2},2e_{3}&gt;\subset M_{4}=M,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{4}/M_{3}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2},\;M_{3}/M_{2}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{4}/M_{3}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2},\;M_{3}/M_{2}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{2}/M_{1}\cong M_{1}/M_{0}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{2}/M_{1}\cong M_{1}/M_{0}\cong\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is easy to see that the restriction of <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to any of these quotients is the identity map, so <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus according to Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the possible cycle lengths are factors of <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this example, there are cycles for each of the possible factors of <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, the cycle generated by <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2e_{1}+2e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2e_{1}+2e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, the cycle generated by <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the cycle generated by <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S4.Thmexample1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second example shows that there may not be a cycle of length <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{b}c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{b}c</annotation></semantics></math> (see Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> for notation) for any <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmexample2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmexample2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmexample2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and any integers <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{p^{a}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the automorphism <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the cyclic permutation of the standard basis <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{1}\rightarrow e_{2}\rightarrow e_{3}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow e_{n}\rightarrow e_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{1}\rightarrow e_{2}\rightarrow e_{3}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow e_{n}\rightarrow e_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the order of <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and the cycle lengths are the factors of <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (for each <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d|n</annotation></semantics></math>, the non-zero element <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+e_{d+1}+e_{2d+1}+\cdots+e_{n-d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+e_{d+1}+e_{2d+1}+\cdots+e_{n-d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a cycle of length <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we use a simplified version of the network given by the diagram of Fig. 2B in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to construct an example. The original network consists of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, the <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> red output nodes that having a single input node are omitted to make the presentation more streamline (these output nodes add very little extra to the computation). The omitted nodes are: FLIP, A20, RANTES, FasT, GZMB, MEK, and LCK. Our purpose here is to give an example from a real world network, finding a good linear model over a finite ring for the underlying biological system is beyond our scope here. We introduce the variables as in Table 1.</p>
</div>
