<td id="S4.T2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{18}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">18</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{18}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-x_{1}+x_{19}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.500em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.500em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">19</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-x_{1}+x_{19}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">20</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{20}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.500em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.500em">12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.1.6.7" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T2.1.6.8" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The update functions of the network given by Fig. 2B in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. The functions are <math id="S4.T2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}=x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T2.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{3}=x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{3}=x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S4.T2.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{8}=2x_{3}+x_{5}+3x_{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>14</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{8}=2x_{3}+x_{5}+3x_{14}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmexample3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix of the above linear system with respect to the standard basis <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{22})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{22})</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Theorem 2.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, the upper bound of the exponent for <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to stabilize (i.e. <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{m+1}(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})=A^{m}(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>22</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>22</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{m+1}(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})=A^{m}(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})</annotation></semantics></math>) is <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22\ln(9)&lt;48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>22</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22\ln(9)&lt;48</annotation></semantics></math>. So we take <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=48</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=A^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=A^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. To find the cycle structure of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to find the cycle structure of the induced linear map (the other part is the kernel of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>):</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A:B(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})\rightarrow B(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>22</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>22</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A:B(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22})\rightarrow B(\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{22}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmexample3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We make the following observation: if <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible matrices over <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UBV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UBV</annotation></semantics></math> is the Smith normal form for <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, we can obtain the cycle lengths of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> from the cycle lengths of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UAU^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UAU^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Note that <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UABV=UAU^{-1}(UBV)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UABV=UAU^{-1}(UBV)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UBV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UBV</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix. Since <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UBV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UBV</annotation></semantics></math> gives the structure of the finite abelian group under consideration, we can read the action of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UAU^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UAU^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from the corresponding upper left submatrix of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UABV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UABV</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. we can just compute <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UABV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UABV</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmexample3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our computation showed that the abelian group generated by the columns of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UBV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UBV</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{9}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and the action induced by <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the following matrix:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;1\\
0&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;1\\
0&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The elementary divisors of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> over the field <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}+x+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}+x+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding orders are <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, according to Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Result ‣ Dynamics of Linear Systems over Finite Commutative Rings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the possible cycles lengths are the factors of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 6=18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 6=18</annotation></semantics></math>. By considering <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{k}B=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{k}B=B</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that the order of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math>. This can also be obtained by computing the order of <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> directly. Thus, the cycle lengths of the linear model are <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,3,6,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,3,6,9</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math>. All the computations can be done using the LinearAlgebra package of MAPLE in less than one second. We remark that the computation can also be done in about the same amount of time for the full <math id="S4.Thmexample3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29</annotation></semantics></math>-node network.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgment</h3>

<div id="S4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Part of the work in this paper was done during the visit of YJW to the Department of Mathematical Sciences at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee in the summer of 2015. YJW wishes to thank the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee and its faculty for the hospitality she received during her visit.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
